17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 32"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",18," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"Containers, Namespaces & cgroups"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"A container is not a tiny VM, it is an ordinary Linux process wearing a disguise"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"In the last chapter we watched a hypervisor conjure whole virtual machines, each with its own guest kernel booting on emulated firmware. Containers look like they solve the same problem, package an app with everything it needs and run it anywhere, but they solve it in a completely different, and much cheaper, way. There is no guest kernel. There is no boot. A container is just a normal process running directly on the host’s kernel, tricked into believing it has the machine to itself."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"That single sentence is the whole chapter, and it is worth slowing down on because so much marketing has made \"container\" sound like magic. It is not magic; it is three plain Linux features you can drive by hand from a shell. Namespaces control what a process is allowed to see. Control groups (cgroups) control how much it is allowed to consume. A packaged root filesystem gives it its own files. Stack those together and you have Docker, containerd, and every Kubernetes pod on the planet."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"This is the payoff chapter for working engineers. By the end you will be able to explain, without hand-waving, why a container starts in milliseconds while a VM takes half a minute, why \"it works on my machine\" mostly stopped being a sentence people say, why a misbehaving container gets killed with exit code 137, and why sharing one kernel is both the reason containers are so light and the reason they are not a security boundary you would bet a bank on. Everything here builds on primitives you already met: PIDs and namespaces, CPU shares, memory limits, and how a process is born, so it should feel less like new territory and more like the moment the earlier pieces snap together."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-containers-vs-vms",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-containers-vs-vms","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"A container is not a VM"}]]}]}],"$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]}]]}],"$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]
